---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SpecCard from '../components/SpecCard.astro';
import { isHeld } from '../data/publish-queue.js';

const held = isHeld('/ccbot/');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="CCBot: User-Agent, robots.txt Rules and How to Verify It | PromptDNS"
  description="CCBot explained: what Common Crawl's crawler does, its user-agent token, robots.txt behavior, how to verify it, and how to block or allow it."
  breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["CCBot"]]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">CCBot</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">CCBot: User-Agent, robots.txt Rules and How to Verify It</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">CCBot is the Common Crawl Foundation's crawler that builds a public web crawl dataset reused by many AI labs and researchers. It honors robots.txt, and Disallow: CCBot excludes your site from future crawl runs. Its fetches resolve to Common Crawl addresses, which is how you verify them.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Crawler specification" data-vs="speccard-wrap">
      <SpecCard
        name="CCBot"
        operator="Common Crawl"
        token="CCBot"
        purpose="open web crawl dataset used by many AI labs"
        robots="honors Disallow: CCBot"
        verify="Reverse DNS resolves to *.commoncrawl.org"
        evidence="Documented"
        verified="2026-10-28"
        href="/ai-crawlers-list/"
      />
    </section>

    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      <h2>How to block CCBot</h2>
      <p>Add this rule to your robots.txt at the root of your domain:</p>
      <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">User-agent: CCBot
Disallow: /</pre>
      <p>Blocking CCBot stops future crawls only. Already-published Common Crawl datasets still contain older snapshots of your site.</p>

      <h2>How to allow CCBot</h2>
      <p>If you want CCBot to crawl your site, make sure no rule disallows it. An explicit allow looks like this:</p>
      <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">User-agent: CCBot
Allow: /</pre>
      <p>Then confirm from the live fetch with the <a href="/ai-crawler-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">AI crawler checker</a>, which reads your published robots.txt the way a crawler would.</p>

      <h2>How to verify CCBot</h2>
      <p>The reliable check is reverse DNS on the fetching IP address. Run the lookup below against any visitor claiming the CCBot token; a genuine fetch resolves inside Common Crawl's address space, and a spoofed one does not.</p>
      <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">dig +short -x <fetching-ip>
# A genuine CCBot fetch resolves to an Common Crawl address</pre>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">The CCBot nuance that surprises people: opting out is not retroactive. Common Crawl publishes periodic dataset snapshots, and content in an already-released snapshot stays there. If that matters to your content strategy, the decision to block is time-sensitive in a way most robots.txt rules are not.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does blocking CCBot remove my site from existing datasets?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. It only prevents future crawls. Previously published Common Crawl snapshots are not retroactively edited.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why block CCBot if I already allow AI search bots?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Common Crawl is a raw dataset license-free to anyone, including model trainers you have never heard of. Some publishers allow search crawlers but refuse blanket dataset inclusion.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I verify CCBot?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Reverse DNS on the fetching IP should resolve to a Common Crawl address.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Related:
        <a href="/ai-crawlers-list/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">all AI crawlers</a>,
        <a href="/how-to-block-ai-crawlers/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">how to block AI crawlers</a>,
        <a href="/how-to-allow-ai-crawlers/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">how to allow AI crawlers</a>,
        <a href="/robots-txt-guide/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">robots.txt guide</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
